<file path="backend/prisma/migrations/20260920_mouvement_equipment/README.md">
# Multi-equipment movements

`Mouvement` is the parent header; `MouvementEquipment` stores each equipment, quantity, optional serial number, source shelf and destination shelf. A header can contain different equipment and different routes. A mixed-direction header has status `MIXED`; each line retains its own direction.

The migration adds the child table, indexes and foreign keys, then copies each existing equipment movement into a child row. Existing header columns remain for compatibility; no history is deleted. The older `EqipmentMouvements` table is unrelated and is left intact.

For a database already managed by Prisma migrations, deploy through the normal migration process. For an existing database without Prisma migration history, apply only this script once from `backend`:

```powershell
node node_modules/prisma/build/index.js db execute --file prisma/migrations/20260920_mouvement_equipment/migration.sql
node node_modules/prisma/build/index.js generate
```

Do not replay `0_init` on an existing schema. This repository's connected database used the second procedure. Direct SQL execution does not create Prisma migration-history records; baseline the existing schema before switching that database to `migrate deploy`.

Both `POST /equipment-movement` with `{ items: [...] }` and `POST /equipment-movement/batch` now return **one parent object with an `items` array**, not an array of independent movements. Each item accepts `equipmentId`, `movementQuantity`, `movementType`, and the applicable shelf/serial IDs. The single-item POST format remains accepted and creates a header with one child. A maximum of 100 lines is accepted. Stock changes and all lines commit or roll back together.

Movement reads include child equipment, serial and shelf details. Equipment history filters matching child lines before computing balances. Serialized equipment quantities retain the existing registration-count semantics: moving a registered serial into or out of a shelf changes shelf allocations, while the global count changes when serials are created or deleted.
</file>

